{
    "word": "mekong",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "b9d5eecb-9cb8-3cd1-923c-2c3505c274d8",
                "word": "Mekong",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                        "entry_id": "b9d5eecb-9cb8-3cd1-923c-2c3505c274d8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A river in southeastern Asia that flows about 4,183 km (2,600 miles) from Tibet to the South China Sea in Vietnam."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4cd56060-ffe4-4309-ba45-d62c227501d5",
                                "sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                                "text": "The Mekong River’s lower basin is vast, encompassing parts of Myanmar and Thailand, virtually all of Laos and Cambodia and parts of southern Vietnam, where, after a 3,000-mile journey across five national borders, the mother of rivers divaricates into a complex delta network and drains into the South China Sea. Tonle Sap Lake sits roughly in the middle of this lush expanse. On a map, it appears as a crooked blue finger extending from the Mekong near Phnom Penh. But it is more often described as Cambodia’s heart, both for its rhythmic flood pulse and the sustaining role it plays in the country’s economy and food supply."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2cafbb4d-c3ca-466b-84d3-ee803ef34b62",
                                "sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                                "text": "The Mekong is one of the largest rivers in the world, beginning in China and flowing through Thailand, Vietnam and Cambodia. Nearly 70 million people in Asia are dependent on its waters for fishing or to irrigate their rice fields."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ffb97d92-369c-4df6-b050-d44f0c2c79b5",
                                "sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                                "text": "In the isolated villages, some of which are only accessible by boat, most Laotians live off the land. There are huge rice paddies on the fertile banks on the Mekong; rice is the Laotians’ main staple, eaten three times a day here."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4c53c09f-9fa9-4faf-98f1-d7bbd9386967",
                                "sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                                "text": "Unsustainable development threatens the health and diverse fish populations of the Mekong river, with one-fifth of fish species in Southeast Asia's main artery facing extinction, a report by conservation groups said on Monday.\nThe Mekong, stretching nearly 5,000 km (3,000 miles) from the Tibetan Plateau to the South China Sea, is a farming and fishing lifeline for tens of millions of people in China, Laos, Myanmar, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ef4116ec-2ac7-4ae6-9292-dccac971081c",
                        "source_entry_id": "b9d5eecb-9cb8-3cd1-923c-2c3505c274d8",
                        "source_sense_id": "97e54eee-d56b-374f-b39f-fef0e009b58c",
                        "source_sense_description": "river in Asia",
                        "target_lang_word": "Mékong",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Mékong",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0fc3d0db-3190-3bcd-b012-f010446a70c8",
                                        "entry_id": "f1fb8b64-fbce-3cd9-9a46-73714efc45d9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Grand fleuve d’Asie du Sud-Est."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Cours d’eau"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "24ba1673-384b-436b-81ac-1413602ce695",
                                                "sense_id": "0fc3d0db-3190-3bcd-b012-f010446a70c8",
                                                "text": "«Les connexions de trafiquants entre les syndicats actifs dans le Mékong, notamment en Thaïlande, et Taïwan sont anciennes et fortes», a expliqué M. Douglas à l’AFP."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "36480f71-8dd6-4867-b325-1c15d17d53d4",
                                                "sense_id": "0fc3d0db-3190-3bcd-b012-f010446a70c8",
                                                "text": "Un pêcheur cambodgien a capturé dans le Mékong le plus gros poisson d’eau douce jamais enregistré d’après des scientifiques, une raie géante de 300 kilos (661 livres)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
